WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
Once the decision is framed, the next question is what the decision is trying to achieve.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.960
Objectives are the outcomes or conditions that matter.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.060
Values explain why those outcomes matter.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.710
Criteria turn those objectives into standards that can be used to compare options.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.820
Without that structure,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.420
teams may appear to agree while each person is optimizing for something different.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.620
For example, one person may want the fastest solution, another may want the lowest cost,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.865
another may want the safest implementation, and another may want the most learning value.

00:00:25.865 --> 00:00:28.260
None of those priorities is automatically wrong.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.550
The problem is that they produce different choices.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.550
Decision science makes the priorities visible so the tradeoffs can be discussed honestly

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.710
instead of hidden inside vague words like best,

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.045
strategic, or efficient.

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:40.505
It helps to separate must-haves from nice-to-haves.

00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:43.860
A must-have is a condition an option must satisfy to remain viable,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.955
such as a legal requirement, a critical safety requirement, or a compatibility constraint.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:51.795
A nice-to-have improves the option but does not define viability by itself.

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:53.400
Confusing the two creates friction.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.920
Teams either reject workable options too early or accept options that fail important

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:58.520
requirements.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.630
Common criteria include quality, cost, speed, safety, resilience, ethics, reversibility,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.020
maintainability, user impact, operational burden, and learning value.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.470
In technical and security work, criteria might include blast radius, detection coverage,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.100
recovery time, auditability, and ability to roll back.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.660
In learning design, criteria might include clarity, accessibility, engagement, transfer,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.860
and assessment quality.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.940
Unclear objectives create false agreement.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.100
Everyone nods until the real tradeoff appears.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.660
Clear objectives do not remove disagreement, but they make disagreement useful.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.720
They let the group ask which objective should dominate in this case,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.200
which tradeoffs are acceptable,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.600
and which option best fits the values and constraints of the decision.
